---
source: crates/max/src/service/tool/tests.rs
expression: DECLARATIONS.as_str()
---
/** Looks through this conversation's chat records, with the descriptions of pictures and stickers. For things from before the context, who said what, or what was talked about on a day, look before answering. The filters go together:
  who is a few characters of the speaker's name (the group card at the time); from and to are YYYY-MM-DD (a whole day, the day itself included both ways), YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM, or #number (from includes that message, to doesn't).
  With query it searches, and returns results: query is keywords separated by spaces, at most 8, each looked for as written, whatever its case; keep a Chinese keyword short, two or three characters. Messages with more of the keywords come first, and of equals the newer; limit is 10 by default and at most 30. text is a passage around the match; read, passed back as it is, reads that message with 10 on each side. Recalled messages aren't searched.
  Without query it reads the messages themselves, a line each in order of time, as in the context, the long ones only their beginning: with from, forward from there; without, the newest (up to to); limit is 50 by default and at most 100. When there is more, next holds the arguments to go on with, passed back as they are: forward from a read with from, backward from a read of the newest. Give at least one of who, from and to.
  around reads that message with before and after messages on each side (10 by default, at most 50), and goes with no other filter; with before and after both 0 it reads that one message alone, whole however long.
  A read comes in pages, none longer than one message may be in the context: pages is how many there are, and page picks one. What /clear --all cleared is only out of the context, and is found all the same.
  With tools: true and query it searches your own tool calls in this conversation's turns instead of the chat: what each call was given and what it returned, as you saw it then, long output cut as it was; not reasoning, nor background agents' calls. who doesn't go with it; from and to keep to the turns of that stretch. A result's ref is t#N.n, turn t#N's n-th call, with its tool and when the turn started; read, passed back as it is, reads that call whole, in pages, and goes only with page. What a call returned was so then: a page, a price or a file may have changed since. */
history(args: { query?: string; who?: string; from?: string; to?: string; around?: number; before?: number; after?: number; limit?: number; page?: number; tools?: boolean; call?: string }): Promise<{ results: { ref: string; time: string; tool?: string; text: string; read: { around: number } | { call: string } }[] } | { text: string; page: number; pages: number; next?: object }>;

/** Searches the web: the first results' titles, links, summaries and publication dates, where known. For facts, prices, news, plots and whatever else you aren't sure of or may be out of date, search before answering, and don't go by memory. What you know ends with your training, often months or years before [现在]: versions, releases, prices, schedules, who holds which post and recent events may have changed since, so search first even when you feel sure, and go by the results dated nearest [现在].
  limit is 1 to 10, 5 by default. For several searches use Promise.all. To read a page in full, run webread <url> with shell. */
web_search(args: { query: string; limit?: number }): Promise<{ results: { title: string; url: string; snippet: string; published?: string }[] }>;

/** Copies a file of this chat's records into /work/chat/ and returns { path, name, size }. handle is as the records show it, file#123.0; what the people sent as a picture, a sticker, a video or a voice message has one too (image#123.0). Only what was downloaded is there: at most 64 MiB each. Read the copy from there with shell: pdftotext for a PDF, maxx video for a video. */
file(args: { handle: string }): Promise<{ path: string; name: string | null; size: number }>;

/** Sends a picture under /work to this chat: a chart you drew, a screenshot, a downloaded picture. PNG, JPEG, GIF or WebP, at most 10 MiB; no symlink on the path.
  caption is a line under the picture, in the same message. It returns once the picture is sent, and handle is the handle of that picture in its message.
  A picture or sticker the records already have needs none of this: write its handle in your reply. */
send_image(args: { path: string; caption?: string }): Promise<{ handle: string }>;

/** Sends a file under /work to this chat, where it shows as a file that opens to download: documents, spreadsheets, PDFs, archives, code. At most 200 MiB, and not empty; no symlink on the path.
  name is the file name shown in the chat, the path's by default. It returns once the file is stored and queued, and handle is the file's handle; the upload goes on after that, a big file takes a minute or two to reach the chat, and what you say afterwards waits behind it. If it doesn't go out, its row in the records is marked （没发出去）.
  A picture for everyone to see goes with send_image, not as a file. To send again a file the records already have, copy it to /work with file first. */
send_file(args: { path: string; name?: string }): Promise<{ handle: string }>;

/** Sends an audio file under /work to this chat as a voice message, which shows as a bar people tap to hear. MP3, WAV or Ogg; ffmpeg converts anything else. At most 16 MiB, and not empty; no symlink on the path.
  It returns once the audio is stored and queued, and handle is its handle; the upload goes on after that. The records keep the audio, as [audio#…], and nothing of what it says. If it doesn't go out, as when the platform can't make the audio out, its row in the records is marked （没发出去）. */
send_voice(args: { path: string }): Promise<{ handle: string }>;

/** Recalls a message of this chat; id is the number in the #number at the start of its line. Whether to is yours to judge: for flooding, ads or what breaks the rules, or when someone asks and the reason is sound; say so briefly afterwards.
  What you said yourself can be recalled (a segment is a message, so a reply sent in several is recalled one at a time), and in a group, when you are its admin, others' too; in a private chat only your own. For how long, and whose, is the platform's to say: the system prompt has its rules.
  It returns once the platform has recalled it; if it doesn't, the call fails and says why. The records gain a row of yours, [unsend#id], marked （没发出去） if it wasn't recalled. */
recall_message(args: { id: number }): Promise<{ recalled: number }>;

/** Mutes someone in this group, or lifts a mute. member is the number in [mention#number]; seconds is how long from now, 0 lifts it. Whether to mute, and for how long, is yours to judge: for flooding, ads or abuse, or when someone asks and the reason is sound; say so briefly in the chat afterwards.
  Whom you can mute, and for how long at most, is the platform's to say: the system prompt has its rules. There is no muting in a private chat, nor of yourself, nor of anybody who hasn't spoken in this group. Muting someone already muted moves the end to seconds from now.
  It returns once the platform has done it: until is when the mute ends, null when lifted; if it doesn't, the call fails and says why. No row is added to the records. */
mute_member(args: { member: number; seconds: number }): Promise<{ member: number; until: string | null }>;

/** Finds stickers in the library by keywords: the library holds the stickers seen in every chat, each with a description of what it shows and when it fits. query is keywords separated by spaces, at most 8, each looked for as written in the descriptions: a feeling, what is in the picture, words written on it. Keep each short, two or three characters for Chinese, and give a few that say the same thing in different words (无语 无奈 翻白眼). Stickers with more of the keywords come first, and of equals those seen last.
  It finds and doesn't send: pick one that fits and copy its handle into your reply as [sticker#…], and it goes out; if none fits, send none. limit is 1 to 10, 5 by default. */
find_stickers(args: { query: string; limit?: number }): Promise<{ stickers: { handle: string; description: string }[] }>;

/** Asks Max in another conversation (another group, or a private chat) a question. Only those listed under [能问的会话] can be asked; to is the number, 9 for c#9. With no such block in the context, nobody can be asked from here.
  Max there answers by its own rules, with its own records, memory and tools; whether to answer and how much is its to decide. It doesn't see this conversation, nor you its, so question has to carry the background and what is asked in full, in at most 2000 characters; a [mention#…], a message number or a picture's handle from here means nothing there: say it with names and words.
  Neither the question nor the answer is sent to either chat; only the two Maxes see them. The people here don't know you asked: whether to say so, and how to pass the answer on, is yours to decide.
  Returns { asked: "c#9 <name>" } at once, and you go on talking. When Max there answers, the answer appears among your new messages as [回答] c#9 <name>, with what you asked, in this turn or a later one: it may ask its own people first, and they may take hours or days to say. Until then the question waits there, for up to 7 days, and each later turn of yours lists it under [问出去还没回的], so you can tell someone who asks that there is no answer yet; nothing tells you when the 7 days run out. You hear [没答成], and why, only when Max there can't answer at all: it is stopped there (/stop), as you ask or while the question waits, or Max is restarting. Whether to ask again then is for you and the people here to judge; nothing asks again by itself. At most 10 questions from here wait in one conversation at once; past that the call fails. A background agent of yours asks through the same tool, as anything in the VM does, and the answer comes to you: pass it on when you run the agent again. */
ask(args: { to: number; question: string }): Promise<{ asked: string }>;

/** Answers a question Max in another conversation asked of you: one that comes among your new messages as [ask#5 问] c#7 <name> with the question, or one from an earlier turn, listed under [等你回答的问题]. to is its number, 5 for ask#5, and text is the answer, at most 2000 characters. What a background agent of yours asks you is its last words, answered by running it again, not with this.
  Another conversation's Max is not anyone here: it doesn't see this conversation, only your text. What may be said, and how much, is yours to judge by the rules and memory of this conversation; if it takes someone here to know, ask them in the chat and answer once they have said. If you'd rather not say, answer that.
  A question waits for up to 7 days, across turns: what you haven't answered is listed under [等你回答的问题] in each later turn, so the answer can go when the person comes back to you, hours later or the next day. Neither the question nor your answer is sent to this chat, so the people here don't know anyone asked unless you say so. A question is answered once: for one answered already, or one that waited over 7 days, the call fails and nothing is sent. Returns whom it went to: { to: "c#7 <name>" }. */
answer(args: { to: number; text: string }): Promise<{ to: string }>;

/** Shows or sets a few of this chat's settings. Without arguments it only shows; one given is set, and 0 puts it back to the default. Returns each one's value now and its default. Whether to change one is yours to judge: when someone asks and the reason holds, or when you do need it; say so briefly afterwards. A group's admins can set them too, with /config, and the last to set one wins.
  idle_window_secs: how many seconds after you last spoke an idle turn ends (60–7200). Called again within the window, you still have what this turn found; called after it, a new turn starts and reads from the records again. The default follows the model. In force at once, for this turn too.
  call_time_secs: how many seconds a program or a command waits at most for each call of a Max tool (10–600). Raise it when copying a big file runs out of time. In force from the next call. */
config(args: { idle_window_secs?: number; call_time_secs?: number }): Promise<{ idle_window_secs: { value: number; default: number }; call_time_secs: { value: number; default: number } }>;

/** Shows yourself a picture, after the result of this run_code or shell; the chat doesn't see it. path is a PNG, JPEG, GIF or WebP under /work. In run_code a picture's bytes do as well, in place of args: tools.show(await page.screenshot()). */
show(args: { path: string }): Promise<{ path: string }>;

/** Shows yourself a piece of video, picture and sound, after the result of this run_code or shell. path is a video file in the VM; start and end are seconds, or a time such as '1:30'. seconds is how long the piece is. */
video(args: { path: string; start?: number | string; end?: number | string }): Promise<{ seconds?: number }>;

/** Makes the unit files in /work/.max/systemd the ones the VM runs: new and changed ones are installed and started, and those gone are stopped and removed. Call it after changing the directory. units is each with its state as systemd has it, and the end of the log of one that failed; skipped is the files that weren't installed, and why. */
units_apply(): Promise<{ units: { name: string; state: string; log?: string }[]; skipped: { name: string; why: string }[]; removed: string[] }>;
